version: "1.2"
abstract: >
  ACS (Adversarial Coordination Spine) is an architectural pattern for the
  multi-agent layer that sits above the Progressive Discovery Spine (PDS) and
  the Model Context Protocol (MCP). It addresses four documented failure modes
  of naive multi-agent deployment: sycophancy collapse from self-evaluation,
  cascading planning errors from over-specified up-front plans, serial collapse
  where models default to single-agent execution, and coherence drift across
  context compaction in long-running sessions. The pattern is defined as
  thirteen principles plus reference SLAs and a numbered build sequence. It is the
  companion spec to PDS: PDS scopes the tool surface of one agent; ACS
  coordinates many agents against that surface.
